Functional Requirements

"Let me state the functional requirements so we're aligned."

FR-1  The board is 3×3 with 9 cells
FR-2  There are exactly 2 players
FR-3  Each player is assigned a symbol — X or O
FR-4  Players take turns placing their symbol on an empty cell
FR-5  A player wins by filling an entire row
FR-6  A player wins by filling an entire column
FR-7  A player wins by filling a diagonal (main or anti)
FR-8  If all 9 cells are filled and no one won → it is a draw
FR-9  A player cannot place on an already-filled cell
FR-10 The game ends immediately on a win or draw



Non-Functional Requirements

"Now the non-functional requirements — these show maturity."

NFR-1  Extensible board size  — 3×3 today, N×N tomorrow
NFR-2  Extensible players     — 2 players today, 3+ tomorrow
NFR-3  Extensible win logic   — add new win conditions without
                                changing Game class
NFR-4  Extensible player type — Human today, Bot/AI tomorrow
NFR-5  Extensible output      — console today, UI/DB tomorrow
NFR-6  Single JVM app         — no distributed locks needed




Identify the Entities

"Based on requirements, let me identify the core entities."

ENTITY              WHY IT EXISTS
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Symbol (enum)       X or O — what a player places
CellStatus (enum)   EMPTY or FILLED — state of a cell
GameStatus (enum)   IN_PROGRESS, WIN, DRAW — state of game
PlayerType (enum)   HUMAN or BOT — for Factory

Cell                One square on the board
                    Knows its position + status + symbol

Board               Holds the N×N grid of Cells
                    Validates and records moves
                    Does NOT decide win — SRP

Player (abstract)   Base for all player types
                    Has shared state: name, symbol
                    Cannot instantiate → enforces contract
  └─ HumanPlayer    Reads move from console input
  └─ BotPlayer      Computes move automatically

WinningStrategy     Interface — checks if a move won
  └─ RowWinning     Checks entire row of last move
  └─ ColWinning     Checks entire column of last move
  └─ DiagonalWinning Checks both diagonals

GameObserver        Interface — reacts to game events
  └─ DisplayBoard   Prints board + result to console
  └─ Leaderboard    Awards points on win
  └─ Notification   Sends alerts on game over

GameEvent           Data passed to every observer
                    Has: message, status, winner

PlayerFactory       Creates HumanPlayer or BotPlayer
                    Caller never uses new directly

Game                Orchestrates the game loop
                    Subject in Observer pattern
                    Delegates win-check to strategies

GameController      Entry point — wires everything

5. Design Patterns — say WHY not just WHAT

"Let me walk through the design patterns I'll use and why."

PATTERN         WHERE                   WHY
──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
Abstract Class  Player                  Has shared STATE
                                        (name, symbol)
                                        Interface can't hold
                                        per-object data

Strategy        WinningStrategy         Win conditions are
                                        pluggable — add new
                                        rule = new class only
                                        Game never changes

Observer        Game → observers        Game should not know
                                        HOW results are shown
                                        DisplayBoard, Leaderboard
                                        listen independently

Factory         PlayerFactory           Centralizes creation
                                        Swap Human→Bot in
                                        one place only


